  "raw": {
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Stevens-Point-WI/Serials-and-Scholarly-Communication-Librarian_JR10016012": {
      "title": "Serials and Scholarly Communication Librarian",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-19\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Serials and Scholarly Communication Librarian Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Job Duties: The University of Wisconsin-Stevens Point (UWSP) Libraries are an innovative partner in the learning community and an active participant in promoting the university’s goals of teaching, learning, scholarship and community outreach. The Libraries provide quality academic services and resources that foster intellectual curiosity, promote critical inquiry and encourage awareness of an increasingly diverse and global environment.   As a tenure-track faculty member, the Serials and Scholarly Communication Librarian provides leadership for the UWSP Libraries’ serials collections, scholarly communication initiatives, and associated budgets. The librarian supports sustainable collection decisions, advances open access and evolving scholarly publishing initiatives, and collaborates with library, campus, and Universities of Wisconsin partners.   The position also contributes to reference, instruction, general collection development, and liaison activities. As a faculty member, the librarian participates in scholarship, professional development, service, and shared governance. Primary Responsibilities: Serials Provide leadership for the acquisition, licensing, access, maintenance, assessment, renewal, and cancellation of serial subscriptions, including managing relationships and negotiating with publishers, subscription agents, and other vendors.  Manage the serials budget and evaluate new and existing subscriptions based on curricular and research needs, cost, usage, accessibility, available funding, and other relevant factors.  Provide leadership and direction to support staff contributing to serials services through training, workflows, and procedures.   Develop and improve serials management practices based on relevant professional standards, technologies, and emerging practices to support efficient workflows, reliable access, and sustainable collection decisions.  Scholarly Communication Provide leadership and expertise in scholarly communication, open access, and evolving models of scholarly publishing.  Provide guidance on publication impact and metrics; evaluate publishing venues and publication integrity; and advise faculty, staff, and students on open access publishing opportunities.    Monitor developments in open access and publisher agreements and collaborate with library and campus partners to increase awareness of scholarly communication and open access opportunities.  Provide guidance and support to researchers on the effective, responsible, and ethical use of AI tools, publisher policies and practices, and intellectual property considerations throughout the research and scholarly communication process.  **Full position description is available upon request. ** Key Job Responsibilities: Department: University Library Compensation: The Serials and Scholarly Communication Librarian position is a full-time, salaried, exempt, annual (12-month) faculty position. Well-qualified candidates can expect a starting annual salary within a range of $49,400 - $60,000 commensurate with the candidate’s education, related experience, and qualifications. Relocation assistance may be available. Eligible Universities of Wisconsin employees receive an excellent benefits package. To learn more about the benefits package, review the  Universities of Wisconsin Employee Benefits Quick Guide  , or  Universities of Wisconsin Employee Benefits Quick Guide  . Use the  Health & Retirement Contributions Estimator  to calculate the estimated value of your compensation and benefits.  Required Qualifications: A master’s degree in library and information science from an ALA-accredited program or equivalent; or, be within a semester of earning master’s degree in library and information science from an ALA-accredited program. Strong verbal and written communication skills. Demonstrated ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a team environment. Demonstrated ability to quickly learn new systems, technologies, processes, and subject areas and apply new knowledge effectively. Demonstrated commitment to diversity, equity, and inclusion principles and practices. Preferred Qualifications: Professional experience working in an academic library environment. Experience negotiating or managing agreements with vendors, publishers, or other information providers, or demonstrated transferable negotiation experience in a related setting. Familiarity working with an integrated library system (i.e., Alma) to manage serials subscriptions, renewals, and electronic resource access. Knowledge of scholarly communication, including open access publishing models, research impact metrics, and copyright/author rights.  How to Apply: TO ENSURE CONSIDERATION: Applications received by 11/16/2026 are ensured full consideration. Applications received after that date may be given consideration at the discretion of the search committee. Application materials will be evaluated, and the most qualified applicants will be invited to participate in the next step of the selection process. Incomplete and/or late application materials may not receive consideration. Files must be complete to be considered. Submission in PDF format is preferred for all attachments. Please include the following documents: Cover letter addressing qualifications and experience Curriculum Vitae Unofficial Transcripts (Official Transcripts may be required of finalist) After you submit your application, you will be unable to edit it. Please be sure to include all required materials before submitting your application. Contact Information: Name: Yan Liao Email: yliao@uwsp.edu Phone: 715-346-3614 Special Notes: Through the discovery and dissemination of knowledge, UW-Stevens Point stimulates intellectual growth, provides a liberal education, and prepares students for a diverse sustainable world. The university is committed to creating a safe, inclusive learning community for all faculty and staff from a variety of backgrounds. Visit https://www.uwsp.edu/join-uw-stevens-point/ for more information about UW-Stevens Point. To learn about the Stevens Point (aka Point) area fun, jobs, housing, education, quality of life, and bragging rights, visit www.stevenspointarea.com . Individuals with disabilities who need a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact Human Resources and Affirmative Action at 715-346-2606 or hr@uwsp.edu. The safety and success of students, faculty, and staff at the University of Wisconsin-Stevens Point is paramount. The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act requires the distribution of an annual security report to all current and prospective students, faculty, and staff. You may obtain the complete report at https://www3.uwsp.edu/protsv/Pages/Clery.aspx The University of Wisconsin-Stevens Point will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except the identity of the successful candidate will be released. See Wis. Stat. sec. 19.36(7). An offer of employment will require a criminal background check and authorization to work within the United States as required by the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Stevens-Point-WI/Assistant-Professor-of-Water-Resources_JR10015973-1": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Water Resources",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-15\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Assistant Professor of Water Resources Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Job Duties: The College of Natural Resources (CNR) has a full-time (9-month), tenure-track, academic-year teaching (100%) position available in the Fisheries and Water Resources Discipline. The position will be focused on teaching, advising, and mentoring undergraduate students in the Fisheries and Water Resources discipline, in particular students in the Water Resources option. Courses the successful applicant will be expected to teach are Water Chemistry and Analysis, Wetlands Ecology and Management, and a team-taught Introduction to Soil and Water Resources. There is the potential to teach other courses in the candidate’s area of expertise, but preference will be given to Stream and Wetland Restoration, Advanced Environmental Analysis, and Applied Ecosystem Ecology. The teaching load for full-time faculty is a minimum of twelve (12) credit hour equivalents per semester. Teaching at least three weeks during the summer with the College’s Summer Field Experience (at the Treehaven Field Station or elsewhere) or co-leading the International Field Experiences in Natural Resources Management for additional compensation is required. Faculty members are expected to accomplish a record of scholarly achievement in their field of interest to complement teaching responsibilities and contribute to college and university governance. Opportunities exist for involvement in research (undergraduate and graduate), outreach, and international programs to meet the University scholarship and service requirements. Experience in and a desire to work with various student learning experiences and backgrounds are expected. Primary responsibilities include, but are not limited to: Teaching (80%) Teach 12 credits equivalent per semester during the academic year. Required courses include Water Chemistry and Analysis, Wetlands Ecology and Management, and Introduction to Soil and Water Resources. Teach a minimum of three weeks of Summer Field Experience or co-lead the International Field Experiences in Natural Resources Management. Research & Scholarship (10%) Scholarly activity or ongoing professional engagement in management of water resources. Secure funding to support scholarship and research activities. Mentor and provide opportunities for undergraduate and/or graduate students to participate in research and/or scholarship activities. Service (10%) Service in the College, University, and/or Professional organization is expected. Key Job Responsibilities: Department: The Fisheries and Water Resources Discipline is in the College of Natural Resources at the University of Wisconsin-Stevens Point ( CNR Homepage ). It has approximately 1600 undergraduates, 50 graduate students, and over 120 faculty and staff. The Fisheries and Water Resources Discipline is currently home to the Wisconsin Cooperative Fishery Research Unit (USGS) and the Center for Watershed Science and Education and works closely with the Wisconsin Department of Natural Resources. The College supports premier undergraduate natural resource programs with disciplines in Fisheries and Water Resources, Forestry, Environment and Society, Soil and Waste Resources, Wildlife Ecology, and the Department of Paper Science and Engineering. Compensation & Benefits: The Assistant Professor of Water Resources is a full-time, salaried, exempt, academic year (9-month), faculty position. Well-qualified candidates can expect a starting academic year salary within a range of $65,000-$72,000 commensurate with the candidate’s education, related experience, and qualifications. Relocation assistance may be available. Eligible Universities of Wisconsin employees receive an excellent benefits package. To learn more about the benefits package, review the  Universities of Wisconsin Employee Benefits Quick Guide . Use the  Health & Retirement Contributions Estimator  to calculate the estimated value of your compensation and benefits.  Required Qualifications: Ph.D. in Water Resources, Aquatic Biogeochemistry, or a closely related field by the date of appointment. Demonstrated experience maintaining and utilizing field and laboratory equipment used for water quality analysis and teaching students. Preferred Qualifications: Demonstrated excellence in undergraduate teaching, including working with students in field and laboratory settings. Demonstrated mentoring of undergraduate students in research and/or career development. Ability to teach courses in Stream and Wetland Restoration, Advanced Environmental Analysis, and/or Applied Ecosystem Ecology. Ability to conduct applied water resources research, as demonstrated by successful publication record and obtaining extramural funding. Collaborative teamwork experience across private, federal, state, municipal, and tribal agencies is desirable. Education: Ph.D. in Water Resources, Aquatic Biogeochemistry, or a closely related field by the date of appointment. How to Apply: TO ENSURE CONSIDERATION: Applications received by 11/15/2026 are ensured full consideration. Applications received after that date may be given consideration at the discretion of the search committee. Application materials will be evaluated, and the most qualified applicants will be invited to participate in the next step of the selection process. Incomplete and/or late application materials may not receive consideration. Files must be complete to be considered. Submission in PDF format is preferred for all attachments. Please include the following documents: Cover letter addressing qualifications and experience Resume/Curriculum Vitae Unofficial/Official Transcripts (Official Transcripts will be required of finalist) After you submit your application, you will be unable to edit it. Please be sure to include all required materials before submitting your application. Contact Information: Name: Jered Studinski, Ph.D., Associate Professor Email: jered.studinski@uwsp.edu Phone: 715-346-2832   If you have any questions regarding the application process, need special arrangements, or if you submitted your application with missing materials, please call or email: Human Resources Email: human.resources@uwsp.edu Phone: 715-346-2606   Please note that successful applicants are responsible for ensuring their eligibility to work in the United States on or before the effective date of appointment. Employment will require a criminal background check. It will also require you to supply a minimum of three (3) professional references (one (1) of which being a current or former supervisor). It is preferred that you supply five (5) professional references (two (2) of which being a current or former supervisors). Your references will be required to answer questions regarding misconduct, sexual violence and sexual harassment. Special Notes: Through the discovery and dissemination of knowledge, UW-Stevens Point stimulates intellectual growth, provides a liberal education, and prepares students for a diverse sustainable world. The university is committed to creating a safe, inclusive learning community for all faculty and staff from a variety of backgrounds. Visit https://www.uwsp.edu/join-uw-stevens-point/ for more information about UW-Stevens Point. To learn about the Stevens Point (aka Point) area fun, jobs, housing, education, quality of life, and bragging rights, visit www.stevenspointarea.com . Individuals with disabilities who need a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact Human Resources and Affirmative Action at 715-346-2606 or hr@uwsp.edu. The safety and success of students, faculty, and staff at the University of Wisconsin-Stevens Point is paramount. The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act requires the distribution of an annual security report to all current and prospective students, faculty, and staff. You may obtain the complete report at https://www3.uwsp.edu/protsv/Pages/Clery.aspx The University of Wisconsin-Stevens Point will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except the identity of the successful candidate will be released. See Wis. Stat. sec. 19.36(7). An offer of employment will require a criminal background check and authorization to work within the United States as required by the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
    },
    "https://wisconsin.wd1.myworkdayjobs.com/UW_Comprehensives/job/Stevens-Point-WI/Assistant-Professor---Clarinet_JR10015196": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor - Clarinet",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-21\nFull time\n\nCurrent Employees: If you are currently employed at any of the Universities of Wisconsin, log in to Workday to apply through the internal application process. Position Title: Assistant Professor - Clarinet Job Category: Faculty Employment Type: Regular Job Profile: Assistant Professor Job Duties: This full-time tenure-track position will include teaching applied clarinet, music theory, and other duties assigned by the chair. The selected candidate will participate in recruitment, retention and advising and be an integral part of a vibrant, collegial department. Key Job Responsibilities: Department: The Department of Music is housed on the UWSP campus in the state-of-the-art Noel Fine Arts Center, built in 2004. Twenty-eight faculty and staff comprise the department, eighteen of which are full-time appointments.  The department has a strong commitment to music education, with a full array of performing ensembles (bands, orchestras, choirs, opera, jazz ensembles, chamber music), and offers the Bachelor of Arts in Music, Bachelor of Music in Music Education, Performance, and Jazz Studies, and the Master of Music Education. Please visit the department web site for more information: http://www.uwsp.edu/music  Compensation: $58,734, minimum, based on qualifications and experience. Required Qualifications: ·         The successful candidate will be a strong classical performer and a proven teacher.   ·         Doctorate in music or minimum Master’s degree in music with 4 years of college teaching experience and active professional performance experience Preferred Qualifications: DMA (Clarinet Performance) University teaching experience Degree or certificate/cognate/minor in music theory. How to Apply: TO ENSURE CONSIDERATION: Applications received by 10/19/2026 are ensured full consideration. Applications received after that date may be given consideration at the discretion of the search committee. Application materials will be evaluated, and the most qualified applicants will be invited to participate in the next step of the selection process. Incomplete and/or late application materials may not receive consideration. Files must be complete to be considered. Submission in PDF format is preferred for all attachments. Please include the following documents: - Cover letter addressing qualifications and experience - Resume/Curriculum Vitae - Unofficial Transcripts/Official Transcripts - A PDF that includes YouTube links with performance examples in contrasting styles (up to 20 minutes).   (Official Transcripts may be required of finalist) After you submit your application, you are unable to edit it, so please be sure to include all application materials. Contact Information: Name: John Lynch Email: jlynch@uwsp.edu Phone: 715-346-3306   If you have any questions regarding the application process, need special arrangements, or if you submitted your application with missing materials, please call or email: Human Resources Email: human.resources@uwsp.edu Phone: 715-346-2606 Special Notes: Through the discovery and dissemination of knowledge, UW-Stevens Point stimulates intellectual growth, provides a liberal education, and prepares students for a diverse sustainable world. The university is committed to creating a safe, inclusive learning community for all faculty and staff from a variety of backgrounds. Visit https://www.uwsp.edu/join-uw-stevens-point/ for more information about UW-Stevens Point. To learn about the Stevens Point (aka Point) area fun, jobs, housing, education, quality of life, and bragging rights, visit www.stevenspointarea.com . Individuals with disabilities who need a reasonable accommodation during the application or interview process should contact Human Resources and Affirmative Action at 715-346-2606 or hr@uwsp.edu. The safety and success of students, faculty, and staff at the University of Wisconsin-Stevens Point is paramount. The Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Policy and Campus Crime Statistics Act requires the distribution of an annual security report to all current and prospective students, faculty, and staff. You may obtain the complete report at https://www3.uwsp.edu/protsv/Pages/Clery.aspx The University of Wisconsin-Stevens Point will not reveal the identities of applicants who request confidentiality in writing, except the identity of the successful candidate will be released. See Wis. Stat. sec. 19.36(7). An offer of employment will require a criminal background check and authorization to work within the United States as required by the Immigration Reform and Control Act of 1986. UW is an Equal Opportunity Employer: Qualified applicants will receive consideration for employment without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, age, pregnancy, disability, status as a protected veteran, or any other bases protected by applicable federal or State law and UW System policies. We are committed to building a workforce that represents a variety of backgrounds, perspectives, and skills, and encourage all qualified individuals to apply."
